 not take any measures at all at the  moment  we don't know in fact we have no idea  new research shows  just one in three people surveyed in the  dutch-speaking part of belgium  still supports the safety measures  that's down from more than 80 percent in  march when the pandemic first hit here  university of ghent professor martin  fanstein gustav who led the study  blames poor official communication for  the dramatic drop in motivation  he says it's been a wake-up call for  authorities to pay more attention to  people's state of mind  i don't think we have sufficiently been  informing people  on the consequences of their behavior  for them  to take a wise decision and now that  summer is over  everyone's back from holidays more  likely to be in enclosed  places at work and school it could be a  toxic coronavirus cocktail  we're quite worried leading belgian  virologist stephen van gupt  says experts are stealing for a  potential september surge  he nonetheless agrees people should be  given more information about actions and  consequences  and then more autonomy so people can  have some freedom  to choose what they think is appropriate  for their situation  and they can adapt their behavior but  are people so sick of restrictions by  now  they won't follow them vanguard believes  most  do comply i don't obey them too strictly  because it's very difficult  but i try anyway i feel much safer  wearing it we must do it all together  but skepticism is clearly growing in  recent days  open letters have been published in  belgian media signed by hundreds of  experts including doctors scientists and  researchers  suggesting the strict measures should be  reviewed  to see if they're stopping the virus or  just the vitality  of everyday life  masks always well let's speak to naomi  rogers who is professor in the history  of medicine  at yale should we be surprised that  people are starting to feel  tired of the masks and the restrictions  no i really think we shouldn't be um i  think that  uh as someone who's also  living through this as well and as a  historian of  disease um i see all around me  a sense that people have responded  pretty seriously um on the basis that  this was some kind of disaster so for a  disaster you know you hunkered down you  you follow guidance and somehow there  was an  implicit uh bargain that it would end  many parents for example worked  extraordinarily  hard with their children um restricting  their movements teaching them online  hoping assuming that come  the autumn that all the schools would be  open and everything would go back to  normal  and it's been a real shock when that is  simply not so  it's like you say there's this bargain  wasn't there if we did as we were told  then we would get an early end to this  pandemic and it's not turned out that  way for people you mentioned you're a  historian  of diseases is there a historic  precedence for this  this fatigue people getting tired of  trying to protect themselves  oh actually during the 1918 pandemic  um and and we don't know as much about  the experience of people  during pandemics as we really should  but it's very clear that people were so  anxious  to um actually to be able to celebrate  the return  of world war one um  veterans and finally at the end of what  we now know was the first  wave of the pandemic there were parades  in the street everybody was delighted  and there was also a feeling that the  pandemic had ended  but in fact that wasn't true and then  there was  um a a real sense of um  anger and frustration when uh  influenza re-emerged uh as a major  uh worldwide uh problem a second time  in just a couple of months after that so  we we do know that there have been  historical precedents both of  the idea of waves and also the  experience  of living through them yeah people are  impatient because they want this to come  to an end  but what does an end look like  how can we say when look this pandemic  is over that's such an  interesting question and i have to say  that in my experience  i've written about epidemics and i've  read widely  about others historians just didn't seem  to really problematize  that question it seems to me it's  actually quite a complicated idea  what is an end who is it who's calling  it in  when does it feel like an end  and in particular for example living  here in the united states i noticed that  um during the summer there's been a lot  of call  for the end simply to be  the end that everybody should be able to  go back to restaurants  and bars um go swimming  in uh lakes in the sea uh just return to  an ordinary happy  summer time essentially they just want  to say  this is the end that's where we are now  let's just pretend  that's exactly they're calling it the  end  um and this has been as you can imagine  resulted in great frustration by many  public health experts who say  well but it but it isn't the end and  people would  people in essence are saying we have  been  uh relatively compliant we have listened  to the science  and you don't know when the end  is um and i think that there is i i mean  i i don't know whether it's so much a  question of communication i think that's  probably one  issue but i think there's also a wider  sense  among the general public and really many  public health experts as well  which is that they don't know um  they don't really have a good sense of  um  what the end is going to look like or  how to more confidently  predict it and when you're experts  real when you realize the experts don't  know  that becomes an even greater um  motivation for just saying well maybe i  i can decide isn't it that's the problem  nobody knows  well at least this interview is now at  an end thank you naomi rogers from the  yale school of medicine it's been great  to get your insights  you're very welcome thank you for having  me  now have you got questions you'd like to  ask us about the coronavirus  well get in contact on our youtube  channel